From: RAX3000Q NSS project
Subject: [PATCH] qualcommax: sysupgrade into the RAX3000Q's single rootfs slot

Upstream groups cmcc,rax3000q with cmcc,mr3000d-ci, cmcc,pz-l8 and the
elecom/iodata boards. That branch assumes the Qualcomm A/B layout:
elecom_upgrade_prepare reads the active rootfs slot from the bootconfig
partition and smempart_next_root resolves the partition to write into,
which needs a "rootfs_1" MTD partition.

The RAX3000Q is single-slot: its flash ends at mtd15 "rootfs", one UBI
holding kernel/rootfs/rootfs_data, and its bootconfig partition keeps the
vendor's own layout. elecom_upgrade_prepare therefore fails, and its
failure handling is nand_do_upgrade_failed, which umounts everything and
force-reboots - so a web-UI sysupgrade restarted the router before a
single byte was written and the old firmware came back.

Give the board its own branch with the plain single-slot sequence the
other non-A/B qualcommax boards use (zyxel,scr50axe and friends): drop
leftover OEM UBI volumes, point CI_UBIPART at "rootfs" and let
nand_do_upgrade flash the sysupgrade tar into the one slot.
nand_upgrade_prepare_ubi recreates the volumes with a dummy
"provisioning" volume in between, so kernel keeps volume id 0 and
rootfs id 1 and the bootargs root=/dev/ubiblock0_1 keep matching the
flashed slot.

---
 target/linux/qualcommax/ipq50xx/base-files/lib/upgrade/platform.sh | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
--- a/target/linux/qualcommax/ipq50xx/base-files/lib/upgrade/platform.sh
+++ b/target/linux/qualcommax/ipq50xx/base-files/lib/upgrade/platform.sh
@@ -419,7 +419,6 @@
 		;;
 	cmcc,mr3000d-ci|\
 	cmcc,pz-l8|\
-	cmcc,rax3000q|\
 	elecom,wrc-x3000gs2|\
 	elecom,wrc-x3000gst2|\
 	iodata,wn-dax3000gr)
@@ -433,6 +432,19 @@
 
 		remove_oem_ubi_volume bt_fw
 		remove_oem_ubi_volume ubi_rootfs
+		remove_oem_ubi_volume wifi_fw
+		nand_do_upgrade "$1"
+		;;
+	cmcc,rax3000q)
+		# The RAX3000Q has no A/B slots: its flash ends at the single
+		# "rootfs" MTD partition, one UBI holding kernel, rootfs and
+		# rootfs_data, and its bootconfig partition keeps the vendor's
+		# own layout. The dual-slot path above needs a rootfs_1
+		# partition, fails and reboots the router before anything is
+		# written, so use the plain single-slot sequence instead.
+		CI_UBIPART="rootfs"
+		remove_oem_ubi_volume ubi_rootfs
+		remove_oem_ubi_volume bt_fw
 		remove_oem_ubi_volume wifi_fw
 		nand_do_upgrade "$1"
 		;;
